# Secret template for tenant.example.yaml (QLabs Catalog Sync, RM-01 MVP).
#
# THIS FILE CONTAINS NO REAL SECRETS AND MUST NEVER CONTAIN ANY -- it is committed to the
# repository so operators have an exact, correct list of variable names. Every value below is
# a placeholder ("CHANGE_ME"); keep it that way in this file.
#
# NAMING IS EXACT AND MUST MATCH THE CONFIG FILE. EnvironmentSecretBackend (the default secret
# backend -- packages/qlabs-catalog-sync/src/qlabs_catalog_sync/config.py, T2.3) resolves each
# `secrets` entry in the YAML config as:
#
#     <ENDPOINT-KEY normalized to UPPER_SNAKE_CASE>__<SECRET KEY upper-cased>
#
# The two endpoint keys in tenant.example.yaml (`databricks_acme`, `qlik_acme`) normalize to
# exactly `DATABRICKS_ACME` and `QLIK_ACME`, which is where the variable names below come from.
# If you renamed the endpoint keys in your copy of tenant.example.yaml (replacing `acme` with
# your own tenant slug, per its step 2), rename the variables below to match -- they must track
# each other exactly, or `EngineConfig.resolve_credentials()` fails at startup, naming the
# endpoint and key it could not find.
#
# HOW TO USE
#   cp deploy/config/tenant.env.example deploy/config/.env
#   # then edit deploy/config/.env and replace every CHANGE_ME with a real value.
#
# `.env` -- that exact name -- is already covered by this repository's root .gitignore (the
# bare `.env` pattern there matches a file with that name at any depth, so `deploy/config/.env`
# is excluded automatically; nothing needed to change there for this template). This example
# file is a different name on purpose and is NOT ignored, so it stays visible in the repo as
# living documentation of the variable names -- which is exactly why it must never hold a real
# value. Never rename your real file to anything other than exactly `.env`, and never fill real
# values into this file instead.
#
# There is no built-in .env loader in the CLI -- these must land in the process environment
# however your deployment does that: `export $(grep -v '^#' deploy/config/.env | xargs)` for a
# manual run, `docker run --env-file deploy/config/.env`, a systemd unit's `EnvironmentFile=`,
# or a Kubernetes Secret projected as container env vars. The "provider abstraction" this
# template sits in front of (`SecretBackend`, same module) is what lets a future deployment
# swap this environment-variable backend for Vault or a cloud secret manager without changing
# the config file's shape at all -- only what resolves `secrets` entries changes.

# --- The credential-store master key (console-configured endpoints only) ---
#
# Needed only if operators enter credentials in the console (an endpoint whose secret
# reference is "db:<endpoint name>"). It encrypts every one of them. ONE key per
# installation, generated once with `uv run python scripts/make_secret_key.py` and never
# touched again when a client is added.
#
# Prefer the FILE form: a file has an owner and a mode, and does not leak into `ps`, a
# container's inspect output, or a crash report the way an environment variable does.
#
#     QLABS_SECRET_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/qlabs-master.key
#
# The inline form is simpler for a laptop or a one-box deployment:
#
#     QLABS_SECRET_KEY=CHANGE_ME
#
# LOSING IT means every stored credential must be entered again -- there is no way to
# recover one from the database alone. Keep it OFF the same medium as your database
# backups: holding both is the one case this encryption does not protect against.
#
# Endpoints that resolve through the variables below instead ("env:<PREFIX>") need no
# master key at all, and the two styles can coexist endpoint by endpoint.

# --- Databricks (databricks_acme) — OAuth M2M service-principal client secret ---
DATABRICKS_ACME__CLIENT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME

# --- Qlik (qlik_acme) — OAuth2 client-credentials client secret ---
QLIK_ACME__CLIENT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME
